WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:07.150
From trash-talking capybaras to a space-bending
casita and Camilo transforming into a giant

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:12.589
Bruno, the early versions of Encanto had some
truly weird and wild storylines.

00:00:12.589 --> 00:00:17.280
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and these
are the surprisingly strange stories Disney

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.210
cancelled in Encanto!

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:22.520
Some spoilers ahead, of course, so take care.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:26.090
TRASH-TALKING CAPYBARAS
One of my favourite animals in Encanto was

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:31.550
Chispi the capybara, whose cool, unbothered
demeanour added a lovely touch of comic relief

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:33.820
to the drama of various scenes.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:39.260
And we nearly got a lot more of the cute creatures
in a very strange storyline as early concept

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:44.600
art for the movie reveals some cheeky, taxidermied
capybaras who liked to trash talk and pass

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:45.810
comment on the family.

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:50.440
In fact, in this piece of concept art, it
looks like one of the stuffed animals was

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:55.410
being taken out of the house, perhaps because
it was making too much of a nuisance of itself!

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:59.410
And I think it would have been especially
amusing to hear their take on the disastrous

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:00.460
proposal dinner.

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:05.930
And it looks like this idea of talking animal
heads would have extended to even more creatures

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:10.950
including turtles, birds, and what looks like
an anteater as we see in this unused concept

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:15.660
art in the Art of Encanto book, which also
has a talking rhino and bear with a fish!

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:20.549
Leave a thumbs-up and comment if you wish
these funny creatures had been in the movie!

00:01:20.549 --> 00:01:24.860
CAMILO IN WONDERLAND
Now, Camilo does a fantastic evil transformation

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:29.601
into Bruno when he describes his uncle as
having a "seven-foot frame" during "We Don't

00:01:29.601 --> 00:01:33.479
Talk About Bruno";
however, Encanto co-director Jared Bush has

00:01:33.479 --> 00:01:38.590
revealed a weird and wonderful animated sequence
where Camilo was telling stories about his

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:44.180
uncle to his friends and as he transformed
he grew to an absolutely gigantic version

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:45.180
of him.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:49.470
This deleted scene has some strong Alice in
Wonderland vibes as the giant room with a

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:54.189
tiny door at the bottom looks very similar
to the room where Alice meets the talking

00:01:54.189 --> 00:01:55.189
doorknob.

00:01:55.189 --> 00:01:58.750
THE INDESTRUCTIBLE PEPA
There's a wacky deleted scene that Disney

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:02.710
released of Mirabel doing chores with her
father and uncle Félix.

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:06.350
Abuela had tasked them with cleaning up the
house while the rest of the magical family

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:09.410
were sent off to perform more spectacular
tasks.

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:12.900
This job in here is the most special.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:14.660
Totally.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:17.480
It totally is.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:21.980
The scene was meant to show the contrast between
the non-magical members of the family and

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:23.099
those with powers.

00:02:23.099 --> 00:02:24.200
Looking great mami!

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:25.980
Watch the rock!

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.190
Thank you Pepa!

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:34.540
You are wel-
Still alive!

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:40.209
In this early version of the story, Mirabel's
aunt Pepa had the power of indestructibility

00:02:40.209 --> 00:02:42.709
rather than being able to control the weather.

00:02:42.709 --> 00:02:48.150
This made Pepa into something of a daredevil,
performing crazy deeds far too dangerous for

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:49.770
any ordinary person.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:54.260
And in that version, it was Mirabel's mother
Julieta who had weather powers.

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:59.099
The problem with the indestructible Pepa though
was that her powers felt too similar to Luisa's

00:02:59.099 --> 00:03:03.849
great strength so that's why, in the end,
she was given control of the weather.

00:03:03.849 --> 00:03:08.800
The deleted scene also reveals a slightly
different take on why Bruno, or Óscar as

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:10.709
he was then known, left the family.

00:03:10.709 --> 00:03:12.210
He said a bunch of stuff.

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:16.340
Abuela got mad, and when Abuela got mad, the
house got mad, and he was like I'm out.

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:19.019
And Abuela was like if you leave this house
you are dead to me.

00:03:19.019 --> 00:03:20.830
And Oscar was like I wish I was dead.

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:21.970
Then he left.

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:26.270
The house getting mad together with Alma is
from an early draft of the story where the

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:30.940
Casita "was an extension of Abuela" and we
would have seen this in things like a room's

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:36.659
wallpaper changing to match Alma's mood and
colours as she moved around the house.

00:03:36.659 --> 00:03:40.330
MAGICAL DOORKNOB QUEST
In a version of the story before they'd even

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:45.720
created the character of Mirabel, after receiving
a mysterious doorknob through the mail, the

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:50.879
film's female protagonist was sent on a quest
to discover the secrets of the Encanto.

00:03:50.879 --> 00:03:55.659
And this later concept art of Mirabel unlocking
a secret door with a magical doorknob has

00:03:55.659 --> 00:04:01.050
some intriguing Coraline and Pan's Labyrinth
vibes about it mixed with Studio Ghibli.

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:05.489
An element of the magical doorknob still made
it into the final film though when, at the

00:04:05.489 --> 00:04:10.299
end, Antonio gives Mirabel a doorknob with
her initial on it which she uses to bring

00:04:10.299 --> 00:04:14.349
the reconstructed Casita back to magical life.

00:04:14.349 --> 00:04:17.829
BAD CASITA
Initial concepts for the magical house also

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:22.629
gave it a more mischievous personality and
it was even antagonistic towards Mirabel to

00:04:22.629 --> 00:04:23.800
begin with.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:27.580
Bad Casita initially tried to stop Mirabel
on her quest.

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:32.540
For example, there was this moment when the
house sent her shooting up into the sky, another

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:36.580
moment where it looks like it blocked her
way through a door by putting another door

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:41.939
in her way, and then there was this scene
where it appears to have flooded her bedroom.

00:04:41.939 --> 00:04:47.080
And in another spooky unused scene, family
members discover Casita is spying on their

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:50.610
private conversation about how they think
the house is crazy.

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:56.520
And in this zany test animation, Mirabel had
to compete with Casita for Abuela's approval,

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:01.830
while the house deliberately sabotaged her
efforts to please her grandmother.

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:06.289
FUNHOUSE CASITA
And Encanto almost had an additional mind-bending

00:05:06.289 --> 00:05:11.659
sci-fi-ish element blended with its magical
realism, giving it shades of Doctor Strange

00:05:11.659 --> 00:05:17.210
or Inception, where Casita would have turned
itself into a funhouse creating these weird

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:22.000
slanting, disorienting corridors and doorways
that folded in on themselves.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:26.189
This idea also reminds me of these trippy
visuals in the third Conjuring movie, The

00:05:26.189 --> 00:05:28.879
Devil Made Me Do It.

00:05:28.879 --> 00:05:32.099
SPACE-BENDING CASITA
In another strangely entertaining version

00:05:32.099 --> 00:05:37.169
of Casita, the space in the house would have
made even less "logical sense", for example,

00:05:37.169 --> 00:05:42.270
in this unused concept art, Mirabel could
hide in this cabinet over on the left but

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:46.689
her arm would e able to emerge magically through
this cupboard on the right so she could sneak

00:05:46.689 --> 00:05:48.469
a pastry or two.

00:05:48.469 --> 00:05:53.050
MIRABEL'S DEEP DIVE
And in another unused Casita storyline, there

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:58.720
was a vast underwater world beneath the house
that Mirabel had to explore, which I suspect

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:01.779
was part of her original quest.

00:06:01.779 --> 00:06:05.830
MAGICAL WALLPAPER
In the end, the filmmakers opted to make Casita

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:10.360
a much friendlier character, and we can see
that change in this unused concept art where

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:15.199
the house helped Mirabel by folding pieces
of wallpaper into magical birds that would

00:06:15.199 --> 00:06:18.749
guide her on her mission to discover the mystery
of the encanto.

00:06:18.749 --> 00:06:23.969
In some other art, it also appears the wallpaper
could act as a portal or door to a magical

00:06:23.969 --> 00:06:26.039
world behind the walls.

00:06:26.039 --> 00:06:31.139
In this art, a door seems to open up in Isabela's
bedroom wall to a place where she could relax

00:06:31.139 --> 00:06:32.169
and be happy.

00:06:32.169 --> 00:06:37.149
Then there's this unused art, which shows
how originally Casita's mood was going to

00:06:37.149 --> 00:06:42.539
reflect Mirabel's mood, for example, here
you can see the wallpaper crying when Mirabel

00:06:42.539 --> 00:06:45.770
feels sad at being left out of the family
fun.

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:50.399
And the house also joined in on re-enactments
of historical battles with Isabela's magical

00:06:50.399 --> 00:06:51.789
potato people.

00:06:51.789 --> 00:06:53.679
More on that later.

00:06:53.679 --> 00:06:57.610
COFFEE WITH CASITA
I love this idea of Mirabel sharing a morning

00:06:57.610 --> 00:07:02.589
coffee with the magical house and putting
hats on the chairs and lamp that Casita communicated

00:07:02.589 --> 00:07:03.589
through.

00:07:03.589 --> 00:07:08.309
We got a little touch of this rejected idea
in the final movie when Casita pours coffee

00:07:08.309 --> 00:07:10.099
for the family.

00:07:10.099 --> 00:07:14.149
LUISA HOLDS UP THE SKY
It's a shame we didn't see inside Luisa's

00:07:14.149 --> 00:07:17.270
room because there were some very cool ideas
for how it would look.

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:22.210
For instance, the room would have had several
large boulders that fell from above and Luisa

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:24.460
had to keep them up with her feet.

00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:28.639
That idea was intended to be "reminiscent
of the myth of Atlas carrying the sky".

00:07:28.639 --> 00:07:34.080
Most of the things in the room appear to be
made of rocks, including the stone bed, but

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:38.469
one especially sweet contrasting detail is
the little puppy Luisa holds.

00:07:38.469 --> 00:07:42.740
And there's some more cute concept art of
Luisa with puppies here.

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:46.729
JUNGLE FURNITURE
As impressive as Antonio's room is in the

00:07:46.729 --> 00:07:50.129
movie, an early idea had an even more surreal
design.

00:07:50.129 --> 00:07:54.919
For example the furniture inside had merged
with parts of the rainforest and the windows

00:07:54.919 --> 00:07:58.979
were portals into the jungle, with waterfalls
streaming through.

00:07:58.979 --> 00:08:03.729
In the end, these ideas were deemed too confusing
to use for the final film.

00:08:03.729 --> 00:08:07.289
COLOMBIAN ROCK
As for the emerald vision tablet that Mirabel

00:08:07.289 --> 00:08:13.039
found in Bruno's room, the original design
was a lot more stylised and its look was inspired

00:08:13.039 --> 00:08:16.979
by the San Agustín statues in Colombia.

00:08:16.979 --> 00:08:20.680
ALMA'S NIGHTMARE
Abuela's trauma from losing her husband Pedro

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:25.819
was a key element of the final movie, but
in an earlier storyline she was also going

00:08:25.819 --> 00:08:31.020
to have a nightmarish vision of some terrifying
ghost-like horse-riders which would have brought

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:34.070
back the horrors of what happened by the river
with Pedro.

00:08:34.070 --> 00:08:39.300
The idea was scrapped for being too scary
and also because it was considered too similar

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:43.560
to scenes with the Druun in Disney's Raya
and the Last Dragon.

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:47.820
THE FORBIDDEN FOREST
Another dark, deleted storyline had Mirabel

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:53.259
venturing outside the safety of Casita and
into what was dubbed a "forbidden forest".

00:08:53.259 --> 00:08:58.380
The off-limits spiky wooded area looks like
it's part of an early version of the movie

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:02.009
where Mirabel was going to discover Isabela's
secret garden.

00:09:02.009 --> 00:09:06.610
In fact, Isabela was incredibly different
in the original Encanto story;

00:09:06.610 --> 00:09:11.339
she had a secret boyfriend who she married
and she could even create living magical potato

00:09:11.339 --> 00:09:12.339
creatures.

00:09:12.339 --> 00:09:16.690
And you can get all the details on that in
my Isabela Deleted Scenes video.

00:09:16.690 --> 00:09:20.000
Tap here to watch it and there's also a link
in the video description.

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:23.720
